ork-level support, but productive double-nitrile hydrolysis under the proposed staging is not established here. Amide accumulation and cyclic amidine or lactam products could delay or divert free glutamate formation.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_014\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Measure cyanohydrin-to-aminodinitrile equilibration, including aldehyde recovery and HCN losses.\",\n        \"Specify acid activity, temperature and residence time for rxn_014 and quantify glutamate separately from amides, cyclic intermediates and pyroglutamate.\",\n        \"Use the verified hydrolysis stoichiometry of four waters consumed and two ammonia equivalents produced; represent ammonium explicitly if using actual acidic solution species.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.61\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.74,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.85,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.73,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.66,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.71,\n      \"feedback\": \"Direct Strecker formation of the stated aminodinitrile is chemically plausible and leads to the correct glutamate skeleton. Its principal unresolved target-forming step is the same double-nitrile hydrolysis used in P9. Imine-first and cyanohydrin-first descriptions can represent overlapping equilibria; neither ordering alone establishes additional experimental validation. Acidic hydrolysis is a reasonable proposal, but conditions and free-glutamate yield are required.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_014\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Demonstrate aminodinitrile accumulation from the declared aldehyde, ammonia and HCN, with substrate and side-product accounting.\",\n        \"Establish a hydrolysis time course resolving nitriles,