displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group. Patel et al. (2015, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202) provides relevant framework-level support, but productive double-nitrile hydrolysis under the proposed staging is not established here. Amide accumulation and cyclic amidine or lactam products could delay or divert free glutamate formation.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_012", "rxn_014"], "suggestions": ["Measure cyanohydrin-to-aminodinitrile equilibration, including aldehyde recovery and HCN losses.", "Specify acid activity, temperature and residence time for rxn_014 and quantify glutamate separately from amides, cyclic intermediates and pyroglutamate.", "Use the verified hydrolysis stoichiometry of four waters consumed and two ammonia equivalents produced; represent ammonium explicitly if using actual acidic solution species."]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_013": 0.8, "rxn_014": 0.61}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.74, "pathway_coherence": 0.85, "environmental_progression": 0.73, "target_achievement": 0.66, "overall_confidence": 0.71, "feedback": "Direct Strecker formation of the stated aminodinitrile is chemically plausible and leads to the correct glutamate skeleton. Its principal unresolved target-forming step is the same double-nitrile hydrolysis used in P9. Imine-first and cyanohydrin-first descriptions can represent overlapping equilibria; neither ordering alone establishes additional experimental validation. Acidic hydrolysis is a reasonable proposal, but conditions and free-glutamate yield are required.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_014"], "suggestions": ["Demonstrate aminodinitrile accumulation from the declared aldehyde, ammonia and HCN, with substrate and side-product accounting.", "Establish a hydrolysis time course resolving nitriles, amides, cyclic products and glutamate.", "Document physically plausible acidification and thermal conditions while measuring recovery between formation and hydrolysis stage