{"timestamp": "2026-10-18T22:37:31.055665", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Glutamate\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\nThe following deep research report contains peer-reviewed literature on prebiotic synthesis\nof Glutamate. Use it as evidence to inform your network design.\n\n=== CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of L-Glutamate: Comprehensive Literature Review\n\n## 1. Known Prebiotic Synthesis Routes\n\nThree experimentally demonstrated pathway families produce L-glutamate (C5H9NO4, SMILES: C(CC(=O)O)C(C(=O)O)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C5H9NO4/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h3H,1-2,6H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10), MW: 147.13) from prebiotically available precursors. These span hydrothermal and surface environments.\n\n### Pathway 1: Iron-Catalyzed Proto-Metabolic Aldol Route (Hydrothermal Environment)\n\nThis multi-step pathway builds the C5 carbon skeleton of glutamate from two smaller molecules \u2014 **pyruvate** (C3H4O3, SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)) and **glyoxylate** (C2H2O3, SMILES: C(=O)C(=O)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)) \u2014 through a sequence of aldol condensation, dehydration, reduction, and reductive amination. The chemistry was demonstrated by Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019, *Nature*, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1) with supporting evidence from Springsteen, Yerabolu, Nelson, Rhea, and Krishnamurthy (2018, *Nature Communications*, DOI: 10.1038/s41467-017-02591-0).\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Aldol condensation**: Pyruvate + Glyoxylate \u2192 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].O=CC(=O)[O-]>>O=C(O)C(=O)CC(O)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 70\u00b0C (343 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Ferrous iron** (Fe\u00b2\u207a, [Fe2+])\n- Mechanism: Aldol condensation \u2014 the methyl \u03b1-carbon of pyruvate attacks the electrophilic aldehyde carbonyl of glyoxylate, forming a new C\u2013C bond that yields the C5 \u03b1-keto acid 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid (C5H6O6, SMILES: C(C(C(=O)O)O)C(=O)C(=O)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C5H6O6/c6-2(4(8)9)1-3(7)5(10)11/h2,6H,1H2,(H,8,9)(H,10,11), MW: 162.10). Fe\u00b2\u207a acts as a Lewis acid to facilitate the condensation. This reaction is also demonstrated under non-iron conditions as part of linked oxidative decarboxylation cycles of glyoxylate analogous to the citric acid cycle (Springsteen et al., 2018).\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Dehydration**: 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid \u2192 4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid + H2O\n- SMILES: O=C(O)C(=O)CC(O)C(=O)O>>O=C(O)C=CC(=O)C(=O)O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 70\u00b0C (343 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Ferrous iron** (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n- Mechanism: \u03b2-elimination of water from 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid, producing the conjugated unsaturated dicarboxylic acid 4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid (C5H4O5, SMILES: C(=CC(=O)O)C(=O)C(=O)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C5H4O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H,(H,7,8)(H,9,10), MW: 144.08). The hydroxyl group at C4 is lost with the adjacent hydrogen, forming the C=C double bond.\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Reduction**: 4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid \u2192 2-Oxoglutarate\n- SMILES: O=C(O)C=CC(=O)C(=O)O>>O=C(O)CCC(=O)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 70\u00b0C (343 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Ferrous iron** (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n- Mechanism: Reduction of the C=C double bond in 4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid, saturating the alkene to yield 2-oxoglutarate (\u03b1-ketoglutarate, C5H6O5, SMILES: C(CC(=O)O)C(=O)C(=O)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C5H6O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10), MW: 146.10). Iron species provide reducing equivalents under the mildly reducing hydrothermal conditions.\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Reductive amination**: 2-Oxoglutarate + hydroxylamine \u2192 L-Glutamate + H2O\n- SMILES: O=C(O)CCC(=O)C(=O)O.NO>>NC(CCC(=O)O)C(=O)O.O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 70\u00b0C (343 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Elemental Iron** (Fe\u2070, [Fe]) and **Ferrous iron** (Fe\u00b2\u207a, [Fe2+])\n- Mechanism: Iron-promoted reductive amination \u2014 hydroxylamine (H3NO, SMILES: NO, InChI: InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2, MW: 33.03) acts as the nitrogen donor, and iron species provide the reducing equivalents to drive the transamination. The \u03b1-keto acid (2-oxoglutarate) is converted to the corresponding \u03b1-amino acid (L-glutamate) at mild hydrothermal temperatures. This is the same mechanism that converts pyruvate to alanine and glyoxylate to glycine, applied here to the C5 keto acid.\n\nThis 4-step pathway is significant because it parallels the reductive citric acid cycle in extant biology, where 2-oxoglutarate is a central metabolic hub. The entire sequence from pyruvate + glyoxylate to glutamate operates under a single set of conditions (70\u00b0C, aqueous, iron catalysts), making it a coherent geochemical pathway.\n\n### Pathway 2: Cyanosulfidic Nitrile Route (Surface Environment)\n\nThe cyanosulfidic protometabolism pathway demonstrated by Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202) produces glutamate through a dinitrile intermediate route.\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Amination**: 2-hydroxypentanedinitrile + NH3 \u2192 2-aminopentanedinitrile\n- SMILES: N#CCCC(O)C#N.N>>N#CCCC(N)C#N\n- Environment: Surface (evaporitic pools, cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic substitution \u2014 ammonia (NH3, SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3, MW: 17.03) displaces the hydroxyl group of 2-hydroxypentanedinitrile (C5H6N2O, SMILES: C(CC(C#N)O)C#N, InChI: InChI=1S/C5H6N2O/c6-3-1-2-5(8)4-7/h5,8H,1-2H2, MW: 110.11), producing 2-aminopentanedinitrile (C5H7N3, SMILES: C(CC(C#N)N)C#N, InChI: InChI=1S/C5H7N3/c6-3-1-2-5(8)4-7/h5H,1-2,8H2, MW: 109.13). This is part of the broader cyanosulfidic framework in which HCN-derived dinitriles serve as amino acid precursors.\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Hydrolysis**: 2-aminopentanedinitrile + 4 H2O \u2192 L-Glutamate + NH3\n- SMILES: N#CCCC(N)C#N.O.O.O.O>>N.NC(CCC(=O)O)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Surface (evaporitic pools, cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature\n- Mechanism: Sequential hydrolysis of both nitrile groups \u2014 water attacks each nitrile carbon nucleophilically, forming amide intermediates that further hydrolyze to the carboxylic acids. The two nitrile groups of 2-aminopentanedinitrile are converted to two carboxylate groups, yielding L-glutamate with release of ammonia. This double nitrile hydrolysis requires four equivalents of water.\n\nThis pathway is part of the broader Powell-Sutherland cyanosulfidic chemistry framework, in which HCN-derived nitriles serve as amino acid precursors. The same unified chemistry produces precursors to RNA, proteins, and lipids from simple feedstocks (HCN, H2S, UV).\n\n### Pathway 3: FeS-Catalyzed Reductive Amination at Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents (Hydrothermal Environment)\n\nKitadai, Nakamura, Yamamoto, Takai, Yoshida, and Oono (2019, *Science Advances*, DOI: 10.1126/sciadv.aav7848) demonstrated that **2-oxoglutarate** reacts with **ammonia** in the presence of ferrous sulfide (FeS) to produce L-glutamate under conditions simulating early ocean alkaline hydrothermal systems.\n\n**Reaction**: 2-Oxoglutarate + NH3 \u2192 L-Glutamate + H2O\n- SMILES: O=C([O-])CCC(=O)C(=O)[O-].N>>N[C@@H](CCC(=O)O)C(=O)O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal (alkaline hydrothermal vent systems)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Ferrous sulfide** (FeS, SMILES: S=[Fe])\n- Mechanism: FeS-catalyzed reductive amination \u2014 ferrous sulfide provides a mineral surface that facilitates the reaction between the \u03b1-keto acid (2-oxoglutarate) and ammonia. FeS acts as both a catalyst and a source of reducing equivalents. The keto group at C2 of 2-oxoglutarate is converted to an amine via imine formation with NH3, followed by reduction on the mineral surface.\n\nThis pathway is significant because it uses ammonia directly (rather than hydroxylamine) as the nitrogen source, which is more prebiotically abundant. FeS is a common mineral in hydrothermal vent systems formed by reaction of dissolved Fe\u00b2\u207a with hydrothermal H2S.\n\n## 2. Key Intermediates and Precursors\n\n### Hub Molecule: 2-Oxoglutarate (\u03b1-ketoglutarate, C5H6O5)\n- Formula: C5H6O5\n- SMILES: C(CC(=O)O)C(=O)C(=O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C5H6O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)\n- MW: 146.10\n- Role: **Central metabolic hub** \u2014 2-oxoglutarate is the key convergence point in the glutamate synthesis network. It is consumed by Pathways 1 and 3 (reductive amination with hydroxylamine or ammonia to produce glutamate), and is produced by at least four different upstream routes: (a) Fe\u00b2\u207a-catalyzed aldol/dehydration/reduction sequence from pyruvate + glyoxylate (Muchowska et al., 2019), (b) oxidation of 2-hydroxyglutaric acid (Lee et al., 2014; Ritson, 2021), (c) decomposition of 2-amino-4-hydroxy-4-methylpentanedioic acid (Lee et al., 2014), and (d) decarboxylation of 3-oxalomalic acid followed by dehydration/reduction (Springsteen et al., 2018). In extant biology, 2-oxoglutarate occupies the same central hub position in the reductive TCA cycle.\n\n### Hub Molecule: Pyruvate (C3H4O3)\n- Formula: C3H4O3\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)\n- MW: 88.06\n- Role: **Upstream hub** \u2014 pyruvate is the C3 building block that undergoes aldol condensation with glyoxylate to initiate the 4-step iron-catalyzed route to glutamate. Pyruvate is a universal metabolic precursor produced by multiple prebiotic routes: CO2 + H2 fixation on native iron/awaruite/magnetite (Preiner et al., 2020), iron-catalyzed decarboxylation of oxaloacetate (Muchowska et al., 2019), and cyanosulfidic oxidation of lactic acid (Ritson, 2021).\n\n### Hub Molecule: Glyoxylate (C2H2O3)\n- Formula: C2H2O3\n- SMILES: C(=O)C(=O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)\n- MW: 74.04\n- Role: **Upstream hub** \u2014 glyoxylate is the C2 building block that combines with pyruvate in the aldol condensation step. It is produced by Fe\u00b2\u207a-catalyzed retro-aldol cleavage of isocitrate (Muchowska et al., 2019), UV-driven photoredox dehydration of 2,2-dihydroxyacetic acid (Liu et al., 2021), and under cyanosulfidic conditions (Ritson, 2021). Glyoxylate also participates in linked cycles of oxidative decarboxylation that are protometabolic analogs of the citric acid cycle (Springsteen et al., 2018).\n\n### Other Key Molecules\n\n**4-Hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid** (C5H6O6)\n- Formula: C5H6O6\n- SMILES: C(C(C(=O)O)O)C(=O)C(=O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C5H6O6/c6-2(4(8)9)1-3(7)5(10)11/h2,6H,1H2,(H,8,9)(H,10,11)\n- MW: 162.10\n- Role: First intermediate in the iron-catalyzed aldol route \u2014 formed by aldol condensation of pyruvate and glyoxylate, subsequently dehydrated to 4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid. Also produced by decarboxylation of 3-oxalomalic acid (Springsteen et al., 2018).\n\n**4-Oxopent-2-enedioic acid** (C5H4O5)\n- Formula: C5H4O5\n- SMILES: C(=CC(=O)O)C(=O)C(=O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C5H4O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)\n- MW: 144.08\n- Role: Second intermediate in the iron-catalyzed aldol route \u2014 formed by dehydration of 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid, subsequently reduced to 2-oxoglutarate.\n\n**Hydroxylamine** (H3NO)\n- Formula: H3NO\n- SMILES: NO\n- InChI: InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2\n- MW: 33.03\n- Role: Nitrogen donor for iron-catalyzed reductive amination of 2-oxoglutarate to glutamate (Muchowska et al., 2019). Could form prebiotically from NO reduction by Fe\u2070 or from NH3 oxidation.\n\n**2-Hydroxypentanedinitrile** (C5H6N2O)\n- Formula: C5H6N2O\n- SMILES: C(CC(C#N)O)C#N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C5H6N2O/c6-3-1-2-5(8)4-7/h5,8H,1-2H2\n- MW: 110.11\n- Role: Cyanohydrin intermediate in the cyanosulfidic pathway \u2014 formed from HCN-derived chemistry in surface pools. The hydroxyl group is replaced by an amine via reaction with NH3 to produce 2-aminopentanedinitrile (Patel et al., 2015).\n\n**2-Aminopentanedinitrile** (C5H7N3)\n- Formula: C5H7N3\n- SMILES: C(CC(C#N)N)C#N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C5H7N3/c6-3-1-2-5(8)4-7/h5H,1-2,8H2\n- MW: 109.13\n- Role: Aminonitrile intermediate \u2014 the penultimate precursor in the cyanosulfidic pathway. Both nitrile groups undergo hydrolysis to carboxylic acids, yielding L-glutamate (Patel et al., 2015).\n\n**2-Hydroxyglutaric acid** (C5H8O5)\n- Formula: C5H8O5\n- SMILES: C(CC(=O)O)C(C(=O)O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C5H8O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h3,6H,1-2H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)\n- MW: 148.11\n- Role: Alternate precursor to 2-oxoglutarate \u2014 the \u03b1-hydroxy acid analog that is oxidized (dehydrogenated) to the \u03b1-keto acid 2-oxoglutarate. Demonstrated under hydrothermal conditions (Lee et al., 2014) and under cyanosulfidic conditions (Ritson, 2021).\n\n**2-Amino-4-hydroxy-4-methylpentanedioic acid** (C6H11NO5)\n- Formula: C6H11NO5\n- SMILES: CC(CC(C(=O)O)N)(C(=O)O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C6H11NO5/c1-6(12,5(10)11)2-3(7)4(8)9/h3,12H,2,7H2,1H3,(H,8,9)(H,10,11)\n- MW: 177.16\n- Role: Alternate precursor to 2-oxoglutarate \u2014 undergoes decomposition (retro-aldol cleavage and deamination) to yield 2-oxoglutarate and ammonia under hydrothermal conditions (Lee et al., 2014).\n\n**3-Oxalomalic acid** (C6H6O8)\n- Formula: C6H6O8\n- SMILES: C(C(C(=O)O)O)(C(=O)C(=O)O)C(=O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C6H6O8/c7-2(5(11)12)1(4(9)10)3(8)6(13)14/h1-2,7H,(H,9,10)(H,11,12)(H,13,14)\n- MW: 206.11\n- Role: Upstream C6 tricarboxylic acid precursor \u2014 undergoes oxidative decarboxylation to produce 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid + CO2, feeding into the aldol route. Part of the linked glyoxylate cycles demonstrated by Springsteen et al. (2018) that are protometabolic analogs of the citric acid cycle.\n\n### Starting Molecules\n- **H2O**: SMILES: O, InChI: InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\n- **CO2**: SMILES: C(=O)=O, InChI: InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\n- **H2** (molecular hydrogen): SMILES: [HH], InChI: InChI=1S/H2/h1H\n- **NH3** (ammonia): SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\n- **HCN** (hydrogen cyanide): SMILES: C#N, InChI: InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\n\n## 3. Reaction Mechanisms and Conditions\n\n### Reactions Producing 2-Oxoglutarate via Iron-Catalyzed Aldol Route (Upstream)\n\n**Reaction U1: Pyruvate + glyoxylate aldol condensation**\n- Pyruvate + Glyoxylate \u2192 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)[O-].O=CC(=O)[O-]>>O=C(O)C(=O)CC(O)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 70\u00b0C (343 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Ferrous iron** (Fe\u00b2\u207a, [Fe2+])\n- Sources: Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019), *Nature*, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1; Springsteen, Yerabolu, Nelson, Rhea, and Krishnamurthy (2018), *Nature Communications*, DOI: 10.1038/s41467-017-02591-0\n- Mechanism: Fe\u00b2\u207a-catalyzed aldol condensation \u2014 the enolizable methyl group of pyruvate attacks the aldehyde carbonyl of glyoxylate, forming the C3\u2013C4 bond of the C5 product. This is the carbon\u2013carbon bond forming step that joins the C3 and C2 building blocks. Springsteen et al. showed this reaction also occurs as part of linked oxidative decarboxylation cycles of glyoxylate without iron catalysis.\n\n**Reaction U2: 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid dehydration**\n- 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid \u2192 4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid + H2O\n- SMILES: O=C(O)C(=O)CC(O)C(=O)O>>O=C(O)C=CC(=O)C(=O)O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 70\u00b0C (343 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Ferrous iron** (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n- Source: Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019), *Nature*, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1\n- Mechanism: \u03b2-elimination of water \u2014 the hydroxyl group at C4 and a hydrogen at C3 are eliminated, forming the trans C2=C3 double bond. Fe\u00b2\u207a facilitates the elimination by coordinating the hydroxyl leaving group.\n\n**Reaction U3: 4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid reduction**\n- 4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid \u2192 2-Oxoglutarate\n- SMILES: O=C(O)C=CC(=O)C(=O)O>>O=C(O)CCC(=O)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 70\u00b0C (343 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Ferrous iron** (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n- Source: Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019), *Nature*, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1\n- Mechanism: Hydrogenation of the C=C double bond \u2014 iron species provide reducing equivalents to saturate the alkene, converting the unsaturated intermediate to 2-oxoglutarate.\n\n### Alternate Upstream Routes to 2-Oxoglutarate\n\n**Reaction U4: 2-hydroxyglutaric acid oxidation (hydrothermal)**\n- 2-hydroxyglutaric acid \u2192 2-Oxoglutarate + H2\n- SMILES: C(CC(=O)O)C(C(=O)O)O>>C(CC(=O)O)C(=O)C(=O)O.[HH]\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Source: Lee, Foustoukos, Sverjensky, Cody, and Hazen (2014), *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*, DOI: 10.1016/j.gca.2014.02.043\n- Mechanism: Oxidative dehydrogenation of the \u03b1-hydroxy group to a ketone, converting the \u03b1-hydroxy acid (2-hydroxyglutaric acid) to the \u03b1-keto acid (2-oxoglutarate) with release of molecular hydrogen. This reaction was studied in the context of glutamic acid stability in hydrothermal fluids.\n\n**Reaction U5: 2-amino-4-hydroxy-4-methylpentanedioic acid decomposition**\n- 2-amino-4-hydroxy-4-methylpentanedioic acid \u2192 2-Oxoglutarate + NH3\n- SMILES: CC(CC(C(=O)O)N)(C(=O)O)O>>C(CC(=O)O)C(=O)C(=O)O.N\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Source: Lee, Foustoukos, Sverjensky, Cody, and Hazen (2014), *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*, DOI: 10.1016/j.gca.2014.02.043\n- Mechanism: Retro-aldol cleavage and oxidative deamination \u2014 the C6 amino acid derivative loses a methyl/hydroxyl fragment and undergoes deamination (releasing NH3) and oxidation to produce 2-oxoglutarate. This degradation pathway demonstrates that complex amino acid derivatives can serve as precursors to simpler keto acids under hydrothermal conditions.\n\n**Reaction U6: 2-hydroxyglutaric acid oxidation (cyanosulfidic)**\n- 2-hydroxyglutaric acid \u2192 2-Oxoglutarate\n- SMILES: C(CC(=O)O)C(C(=O)O)O>>C(CC(=O)O)C(=O)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Ritson (2021), *Science Advances*, DOI: 10.1126/sciadv.abh3981\n- Mechanism: Oxidation of the \u03b1-hydroxy group to a ketone under cyanosulfidic conditions, where sulfur species (derived from H2S/cyanosulfidic chemistry) act as the oxidant. This provides a surface-environment route to 2-oxoglutarate as part of a prebiotic analog of the Krebs cycle.\n\n**Reaction U7: 3-oxalomalic acid decarboxylation**\n- 3-oxalomalic acid \u2192 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid + CO2\n- SMILES: C(C(C(=O)O)O)(C(=O)C(=O)O)C(=O)O>>C(C(C(=O)[O-])O)C(=O)C(=O)[O-].C(=O)=O\n- Environment: Surface\n- Temperature: 23\u00b0C (296 K)\n- Time: 3 hours\n- Sources: Springsteen, Yerabolu, Nelson, Rhea, and Krishnamurthy (2018), *Nature Communications*, DOI: 10.1038/s41467-017-02591-0\n- Mechanism: Oxidative decarboxylation \u2014 the C6 tricarboxylic acid 3-oxalomalic acid loses CO2 from its oxalyl group, producing the C5 intermediate 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid. This reaction proceeds at ambient temperature (23\u00b0C) over 3 hours and is part of the linked glyoxylate cycles that are protometabolic analogs of the citric acid cycle.\n\n### Upstream Reaction for Cyanosulfidic Pathway\n\n**Reaction U8: 2-hydroxypentanedinitrile amination**\n- 2-hydroxypentanedinitrile + NH3 \u2192 2-aminopentanedinitrile\n- SMILES: N#CCCC(O)C#N.N>>N#CCCC(N)C#N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic substitution \u2014 ammonia displaces the hydroxyl group at C2 of the dinitrile, converting the cyanohydrin to the aminonitrile. This is part of the broader Strecker-type chemistry operating under cyanosulfidic conditions.\n\n### Reactions Producing L-Glutamate (Final Steps)\n\n**Reaction F1: Reductive amination with hydroxylamine (iron-catalyzed)**\n- 2-Oxoglutarate + hydroxylamine \u2192 L-Glutamate + 2 H2O\n- SMILES: O=C(O)CCC(=O)C(=O)O.NO>>NC(CCC(=O)O)C(=O)O.O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal\n- Temperature: 70\u00b0C (343 K)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Elemental Iron** (Fe\u2070, [Fe]) and **Ferrous iron** (Fe\u00b2\u207a, [Fe2+])\n- Source: Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019), *Nature*, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1\n- Mechanism: Iron-promoted reductive amination \u2014 hydroxylamine acts as the nitrogen donor, and iron species provide the reducing equivalents to drive the transamination. The \u03b1-keto group of 2-oxoglutarate is converted to an \u03b1-amino group, yielding glutamate.\n\n**Reaction F2: Dinitrile hydrolysis (cyanosulfidic)**\n- 2-aminopentanedinitrile + 4 H2O \u2192 L-Glutamate + NH3\n- SMILES: N#CCCC(N)C#N.O.O.O.O>>N.NC(CCC(=O)O)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Sequential nitrile hydrolysis \u2014 each of the two nitrile groups (C#N) undergoes nucleophilic attack by water, forming amide intermediates that further hydrolyze to carboxylic acids (COOH). This converts the dinitrile carbon skeleton into glutamate with release of one equivalent of NH3.\n\n**Reaction F3: Reductive amination with ammonia (FeS-catalyzed)**\n- 2-Oxoglutarate + NH3 \u2192 L-Glutamate + H2O\n- SMILES: O=C([O-])CCC(=O)C(=O)[O-].N>>N[C@@H](CCC(=O)O)C(=O)O.O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal (alkaline hydrothermal vent systems)\n- Mineral catalysts: **Ferrous sulfide** (FeS, S=[Fe])\n- Source: Kitadai, Nakamura, Yamamoto, Takai, Yoshida, and Oono (2019), *Science Advances*, DOI: 10.1126/sciadv.aav7848\n- Mechanism: FeS surface-catalyzed reductive amination \u2014 ammonia reacts with the \u03b1-keto group of 2-oxoglutarate on the FeS mineral surface. The mineral provides both a catalytic surface for the imine intermediate formation and reducing equivalents for the final reduction step.\n\n## 4. Mineral Catalysts and Surfaces\n\nThe following mineral catalysts and metal species are experimentally documented for reactions in the L-glutamate synthesis network:\n\n- **Elemental Iron (Fe\u2070)**: Highly reactive, insoluble metallic iron (SMILES: [Fe]). Acts as a strong reductant and drives reductive amination of 2-oxoglutarate to glutamate with hydroxylamine. Not soluble; very reactive. Used in: 2-oxoglutarate-to-glutamate reductive amination (Muchowska et al., 2019).\n\n- **Ferrous iron (Fe\u00b2\u207a)**: Soluble iron(II) species (SMILES: [Fe2+]), abundant in anoxic early Earth oceans. Converts to insoluble Fe\u00b3\u207a in the presence of oxygen (not relevant on anoxic early Earth). Acts as Lewis acid catalyst for aldol condensation, dehydration, and reduction reactions. Used in: pyruvate-glyoxylate aldol condensation (Muchowska et al., 2019; Springsteen et al., 2018), 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid dehydration (Muchowska et al., 2019), 4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid reduction (Muchowska et al., 2019), and 2-oxoglutarate-to-glutamate reductive amination (Muchowska et al., 2019).\n\n- **Ferrous sulfide (FeS)**: Iron(II) sulfide mineral (SMILES: S=[Fe]), common in hydrothermal vent systems where dissolved Fe\u00b2\u207a reacts with hydrothermal H2S. Provides a mineral surface for catalysis of reductive amination reactions. Used in: 2-oxoglutarate + ammonia \u2192 glutamate reductive amination at alkaline hydrothermal vents (Kitadai et al., 2019).\n\n## 5. Experimental Evidence\n\n### Study 1: Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019)\n- **Title**: \"Synthesis and breakdown of universal metabolic precursors promoted by iron\"\n- **Journal**: Nature\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that metallic iron (Fe\u2070) and ferrous iron (Fe\u00b2\u207a) at 70\u00b0C in water promote an interconnected network of reactions analogous to the core of biological metabolism. For glutamate specifically, this includes the complete 4-step pathway: (a) aldol condensation of pyruvate + glyoxylate \u2192 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid, (b) dehydration \u2192 4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid, (c) reduction \u2192 2-oxoglutarate, and (d) reductive amination with hydroxylamine \u2192 glutamate. This establishes a simple geochemical analog of the reductive TCA cycle and amino acid biosynthesis driven by a single metal catalyst system.\n\n### Study 2: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015)\n- **Title**: \"Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism\"\n- **Journal**: Nature Chemistry\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that a unified cyanosulfidic chemistry starting from HCN and H2S can produce precursors to RNA, proteins, and lipids. For glutamate specifically, 2-hydroxypentanedinitrile is converted to 2-aminopentanedinitrile by reaction with NH3, and the dinitrile hydrolyzes to glutamate in aqueous conditions. This provides a surface-environment route to glutamate through Strecker-type chemistry.\n\n### Study 3: Springsteen, Yerabolu, Nelson, Rhea, and Krishnamurthy (2018)\n- **Title**: \"Linked cycles of oxidative decarboxylation of glyoxylate as protometabolic analogs of the citric acid cycle\"\n- **Journal**: Nature Communications\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/s41467-017-02591-0\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated linked cycles of oxidative decarboxylation of glyoxylate that produce intermediates analogous to those of the citric acid cycle without enzymes. Relevant to glutamate synthesis: (a) 3-oxalomalic acid undergoes decarboxylation to 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid + CO2 at 23\u00b0C over 3 hours, and (b) pyruvate + glyoxylate undergo aldol condensation to produce 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid. These reactions provide non-enzymatic routes to the key C5 intermediates in the glutamate synthesis network.\n\n### Study 4: Kitadai, Nakamura, Yamamoto, Takai, Yoshida, and Oono (2019)\n- **Title**: \"Metals likely promoted protometabolism in early ocean alkaline hydrothermal systems\"\n- **Journal**: Science Advances\n- **DOI**: 10.1126/sciadv.aav7848\n- **Key finding**: Showed that metal sulfide minerals (including FeS) at alkaline hydrothermal vents can catalyze reductive amination of \u03b1-keto acids to \u03b1-amino acids. For glutamate specifically, FeS catalyzes the reaction of 2-oxoglutarate with ammonia to produce glutamate. This provides a direct, single-step route to glutamate from the central metabolic hub 2-oxoglutarate using a prebiotically common mineral and nitrogen source.\n\n### Study 5: Lee, Foustoukos, Sverjensky, Cody, and Hazen (2014)\n- **Title**: \"The effects of temperature, pH and redox state on the stability of glutamic acid in hydrothermal fluids\"\n- **Journal**: Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta\n- **DOI**: 10.1016/j.gca.2014.02.043\n- **Key finding**: Investigated the stability and decomposition/interconversion of glutamic acid and related molecules in hydrothermal fluids. Demonstrated that: (a) 2-hydroxyglutaric acid can be oxidized to 2-oxoglutarate with release of H2, and (b) 2-amino-4-hydroxy-4-methylpentanedioic acid decomposes to 2-oxoglutarate + NH3. These reactions provide additional hydrothermal routes to the central hub molecule 2-oxoglutarate and illuminate the equilibrium chemistry of glutamate in hot aqueous environments.\n\n### Study 6: Ritson (2021)\n- **Title**: \"A cyanosulfidic origin of the Krebs cycle\"\n- **Journal**: Science Advances\n- **DOI**: 10.1126/sciadv.abh3981\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that a cyanosulfidic reaction network can produce the intermediates of the Krebs (TCA) cycle abiotically. Relevant to glutamate synthesis: 2-hydroxyglutaric acid is oxidized to 2-oxoglutarate under cyanosulfidic surface conditions, providing a surface-environment route to the central hub molecule that complements the hydrothermal iron-catalyzed pathway.\n\n## 6. Environmental Context\n\n### Hydrothermal Environment (Primary for Pathways 1 and 3)\nThe majority of experimentally demonstrated glutamate synthesis routes operate under hydrothermal conditions:\n- **Iron-catalyzed aldol/dehydration/reduction/amination** (Muchowska et al., 2019): The complete 4-step sequence from pyruvate + glyoxylate \u2192 glutamate operates at 70\u00b0C with Fe\u2070/Fe\u00b2\u207a. This mimics conditions at alkaline hydrothermal vents where metallic iron and dissolved Fe\u00b2\u207a are available from serpentinization, and temperatures of 70\u00b0C are found in the mixing zones between hot vent fluid and cooler ocean water.\n- **FeS-catalyzed reductive amination** (Kitadai et al., 2019): 2-oxoglutarate + NH3 \u2192 glutamate on ferrous sulfide surfaces. FeS is naturally formed in hydrothermal systems where dissolved Fe\u00b2\u207a meets H2S.\n- **Hydrothermal stability studies** (Lee et al., 2014): 2-hydroxyglutaric acid and 2-amino-4-hydroxy-4-methylpentanedioic acid decompose to 2-oxoglutarate in hydrothermal fluids, demonstrating that the keto acid hub molecule is a thermodynamically stable product under these conditions.\n- **Pyruvate** is produced by CO2 + H2 fixation on native iron minerals at 100\u00b0C (Preiner et al., 2020) and by Fe\u00b2\u207a-catalyzed decarboxylation of oxaloacetate at 70\u00b0C (Muchowska et al., 2019).\n- **Glyoxylate** is produced by Fe\u00b2\u207a-catalyzed retro-aldol cleavage of isocitrate at 70\u00b0C (Muchowska et al., 2019).\n\n### Surface Environment (Primary for Pathway 2)\n- **Cyanosulfidic dinitrile route** (Patel et al., 2015): 2-hydroxypentanedinitrile \u2192 2-aminopentanedinitrile \u2192 glutamate. This operates in surface pools where HCN-derived chemistry (UV-driven, evaporitic) produces dinitrile intermediates that undergo amination and hydrolysis.\n- **Cyanosulfidic oxidation** (Ritson, 2021): 2-hydroxyglutaric acid is oxidized to 2-oxoglutarate under surface cyanosulfidic conditions as part of a prebiotic analog of the Krebs cycle.\n- **Glyoxylate oxidative decarboxylation cycles** (Springsteen et al., 2018): 3-oxalomalic acid decarboxylates to 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid + CO2 at 23\u00b0C over 3 hours in aqueous solution, demonstrating that key intermediates can form under ambient surface conditions.\n\n### Environmental Interconnection\nThe synthesis network exhibits clear cross-environment flow:\n1. **Hydrothermal \u2192 Surface**: 2-Oxoglutarate and other metabolic intermediates generated at hydrothermal vents could be transported to surface environments via ocean circulation, where they undergo cyanosulfidic transformations or accumulate in evaporitic pools.\n2. **Surface \u2192 Hydrothermal**: Glyoxylate produced by UV photochemistry in surface pools could be washed into hydrothermal zones where it participates in the iron-catalyzed aldol condensation with pyruvate. Similarly, 2-hydroxyglutaric acid produced by surface cyanosulfidic chemistry could be transported to hydrothermal vents where it is oxidized to 2-oxoglutarate.\n3. **Both \u2192 Biochemical**: L-Glutamate produced by any pathway enters the prebiotic amino acid pool available for peptide bond formation, proto-metabolic assembly, and as a nitrogen carrier in transamination reactions (glutamate is the primary nitrogen donor in extant metabolism).\n\n## 7. Open Questions and Challenges\n\n1. **Chirality**: All demonstrated prebiotic routes produce racemic glutamate (equal L and D). The origin of homochirality (biological preference for L-amino acids) remains unresolved. Mineral surface adsorption, crystallization, or photolysis with circularly polarized light have been proposed but not definitively demonstrated for glutamate.\n\n2. **Hydroxylamine availability**: The Muchowska et al. iron-catalyzed pathway (Pathway 1, Step 4) requires hydroxylamine (NH2OH), whose prebiotic abundance is debated. It could form from NO reduction by Fe\u2070 or from NH3 oxidation by reactive oxygen species, but quantitative constraints are needed. The FeS-catalyzed pathway (Pathway 3) circumvents this by using ammonia directly.\n\n3. **2-Oxoglutarate thermal stability**: While Lee et al. (2014) showed that 2-oxoglutarate is a thermodynamically stable product of several decomposition reactions in hydrothermal fluids, \u03b1-keto acids can also undergo decarboxylation at elevated temperatures. The kinetic competition between 2-oxoglutarate production (from upstream reactions) and its decomposition (to succinate semialdehyde + CO2) under sustained hydrothermal conditions is a key question.\n\n4. **Multi-step pathway efficiency**: The 4-step iron-catalyzed aldol route (Pathway 1) requires all intermediates (4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid, 4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid, 2-oxoglutarate) to accumulate to sufficient concentrations at 70\u00b0C. Side reactions, dilution, and competing decomposition pathways could reduce overall yield. The relative flux through this pathway versus the single-step FeS route (Pathway 3) is unknown.\n\n5. **Dinitrile intermediate stability**: The cyanosulfidic pathway (Pathway 2) requires 2-hydroxypentanedinitrile and 2-aminopentanedinitrile to persist long enough for amination and hydrolysis respectively. The stability of these dinitriles under UV irradiation, varying pH, and temperature fluctuations in surface pools has not been fully characterized for the C5 species.\n\n6. **FeS versus Fe\u2070/Fe\u00b2\u207a selectivity**: Two different mineral systems catalyze the final reductive amination step \u2014 Fe\u2070/Fe\u00b2\u207a with hydroxylamine (Muchowska et al., 2019) and FeS with ammonia (Kitadai et al., 2019). The relative efficiencies, selectivities, and yields of these two systems for glutamate production under realistic prebiotic conditions remain to be compared directly.\n\n7. **Glyoxylate cycle completeness**: The Springsteen et al. (2018) demonstration of linked glyoxylate decarboxylation cycles produces 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid as an intermediate, but the full cycle connecting these reactions to the iron-catalyzed downstream chemistry has not been demonstrated in a single continuous experiment. Whether these surface-temperature reactions (23\u00b0C) can feed productively into the hydrothermal-temperature reactions (70\u00b0C) is an open question.\n\n=== END CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.76\n- Feedback: This is a connected, sufficiently populated, chemically plausible candidate network, but not an experimentally established complete synthesis network. All 21 molecule nodes and 14 reaction nodes participate in the connected graph. Under the required deterministic ground truth, every reaction is balanced or balanceable with small coefficients; none is a conservation violation. Store the inferred coefficients explicitly, especially the two Fe and four protons in rxn_008 and the four waters and two ammonia products in rxn_014. The reported mol_015 formula/InChI discrepancy is a metadata issue requiring correction to one Fe atom with formal charge +2, for example SMILES [Fe+2]; it does not overturn the supplied reaction-balance results. The declared feedstock boundary is acceptable, and racemic glutamate satisfies the stated target. The network appropriately distinguishes sequence variants from independently demonstrated synthesis families, accounts for sacrificial reductions, and corrects misleading review descriptions of oxime reduction, nonoxidative decarboxylation, and nitrile-hydrolysis stoichiometry. Literature alignment is meaningful but incomplete: the supplied review supports several reaction families, while the network itself supplies no reaction-specific citations, numerical protocols, or yields establishing the exact implementations. Critical uncertainties are selective rxn_007 reduction, H2 activation and reductive amination on the proposed FeS interface in rxn_009, acrolein regioselectivity in rxn_010, and productive hydrolysis conditions for rxn_014. These uncertainties must be assessed across the complete routes rather than averaged away by additional entry sequences. Surface heating, anoxia, UV shielding, and staged processing are physically plausible, but catalyst speciation, passivation, pH, temperature, residence times, transport, dilution, and intermediate survival remain insufficiently specified. End-to-end evidence is needed that intermediates accumulate and remain compatible with subsequent stages. P1 is the strongest candidate, but the complete network does not reach the 0.90 approval threshold.\n- Issues: ['The carbon-skeleton route is coherent, but target formation depends on the explicitly conditional rxn_009. Adding H2 correctly supplies the net reducing equivalents; it does not establish that the specified FeS interface activates H2 and reduces the imine at a useful rate. The supplied Kitadai summary is insufficient to validate this exact implementation. Delivery and survival of surface-produced 2-oxoglutarate at the hydrothermal interface are also unspecified.', 'The aggregate CO2/H2 entry is balanced and has literature precedent at the transformation-family level through the Preiner work cited in the review. It is acceptable as a declared aggregate transformation, but an unspecified mineral and hydrothermal label do not establish pyruvate production under the proposed conditions. The downstream hydroxylamine completion is credible, while pyruvate recovery, transfer, and the selectivity of rxn_007 remain unresolved.', 'The sequence conserves atoms and charge and connects the declared feedstocks to glutamate, but both its inorganic-carbon entry and terminal H2/FeS amination lack implementation-specific validation. The environmental concern is not the order or number of environments; it is the absence of demonstrated transport, retention, and chemical compatibility for the intermediates. The shared selective-reduction uncertainty in rxn_007 remains.', 'The oxaloacetate-to-pyruvate entry is sound, but it does not resolve the downstream uncertainties in selective iron reduction and H2/FeS reductive amination. The final step is stoichiometrically correct but remains conditional rather than demonstrated by the submitted evidence. Hydrothermal processing also requires a suitable residence time to avoid losing 2-oxoglutarate before productive amination.', \"Oxaloacetate-glyoxylate aldol addition followed by beta-keto-acid decarboxylation provides a chemically coherent C5 entry. Describing rxn_005 as nonoxidative is appropriate and avoids the review's misleading oxidative label. The Springsteen review supports the entry chemistry, but productive aldol capture must compete with oxaloacetate decarboxylation and other reactions. Downstream selective reduction and complete-sequence yield remain unverified.\", 'The carbon-skeleton sequence is plausible and conservation-valid, including nonoxidative rxn_005. Nevertheless, selective oxalomalate formation, selective alkene reduction, and terminal H2/FeS amination each need implementation-specific evidence. Explicitly calling the final step conditional is scientifically appropriate, but does not demonstrate target production. Transfer to a compatible hydrothermal reaction zone is also not physically specified.', 'The dinitrile skeleton would hydrolyze to glutamate, and rxn_012 is reasonably interpreted as cyanohydrin-mediated Strecker equilibration rather than simple displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group. The weak entry is selective conjugate cyanation of acrolein: carbonyl cyanohydrin formation and polymerization compete. The Patel review supports related precursor chemistry, but does not by itself validate this acrolein implementation or rapid double-nitrile hydrolysis under unspecified surface conditions.', 'Direct Strecker capture of authenticated 3-cyanopropanal is chemically reasonable and does not require an accumulated cyanohydrin pool. However, production of that aldehyde by selective acrolein conjugate cyanation is still unvalidated in the supplied implementation. Double-nitrile hydrolysis has the correct target connectivity and stoichiometry, but needs an explicit catalytic and thermal regime; a surface-environment label alone does not establish a useful hydrolysis rate.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.73\n- Feedback: The network is a connected, stoichiometrically valid and chemically motivated proposal, but it is not approved at the required network confidence threshold of 0.90. Complete-network criterion scores are: network validity 1.00, chemical feasibility 0.73, environmental consistency 0.75, pathway coherence 0.84, target achievement 0.69, and literature alignment 0.72. All 20 molecule nodes and 14 reaction nodes participate in one connected graph, and the deterministic check establishes no mass or charge violations. The four coefficient-bearing reactions require only small, reasonable coefficients, which should be written explicitly. Problematic-reaction flags concern kinetics, selectivity, operating conditions or evidence, not conservation. The main target-production uncertainties are selective Fe0 reduction in rxn_007, the proposed Fe0/ammonia implementation in rxn_010, and productive free-glutamate formation in rxn_014. Confidence reflects these unresolved transformations rather than the number of pathway variants or convergence points. Surface geothermal water can accommodate 343 K chemistry, and separate pH stages are legitimate, but reaction-specific pH, residence times, concentrations, iron reactivity and stage-to-stage recovery are not supplied. The declared carbon-feedstock boundary is accepted; synthesis from inorganic carbon is not required. Explicit sacrificial iron, nonoxidative rxn_005, correct ammonia stoichiometry in rxn_014 and the racemic target appropriately correct weaknesses in the supplied review. Nevertheless, reaction-level citations and primary experimental protocols are absent from the reaction rationales, and framework-level literature support does not validate every proposed implementation. Demonstrating at least one productive end-to-end sequence with authenticated intermediates, carbon recovery and quantified glutamate would substantially strengthen the network.\n- Issues: ['The carbon connectivity, oxime formation and reductive conversion to racemic glutamate are coherent. Muchowska et al. (2019, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1) supports the broader iron-promoted chemistry, but the supplied evidence does not establish selective reduction of mol_006 using this particular sacrificial-iron implementation. Competitive carbonyl reduction, decomposition and iron corrosion remain important. A 343 K surface geothermal setting is physically compatible with aqueous chemistry, but reaction-specific operating conditions are missing.', 'The route is balanced and has the correct carbon skeleton, but both selective alkene reduction and the specified Fe0/ammonia reductive amination require validation. A balanced electron budget does not establish productive imine formation or selective imine reduction in water. The Kitadai et al. citation (2019, DOI: 10.1126/sciadv.aav7848) should be checked for the actual active material, substrate and conditions rather than treated as automatic support for this implementation.', 'Oxaloacetate decarboxylation is a chemically credible source of pyruvate and connects correctly to the aldol sequence. This entry transformation does not resolve the shared uncertainty in rxn_007. The hydroxylamine completion is comparatively well supported, and the neutral reference forms and racemic target are appropriate. Useful flux requires controlling oxaloacetate consumption and retaining the downstream keto-acid intermediates.', 'The oxaloacetate-to-pyruvate entry is sound, and all listed transformations conserve atoms and charge with the supplied coefficients. Target production nevertheless depends on two insufficiently established selective reductions. The proposed ammonia branch cannot inherit the experimental support of hydroxylamine-mediated amination without demonstrating the different nitrogen-incorporation chemistry.', 'The oxalomalate entry has plausible connectivity and relevant support from Springsteen et al. (2018, DOI: 10.1038/s41467-017-02591-0). Describing rxn_005 as nonoxidative decarboxylation is chemically appropriate for the stated transformation; no oxidant is missing. Productive aldol capture must compete with oxaloacetate decarboxylation and reversible carbon-carbon chemistry. The downstream selective Fe0 reduction remains the principal unresolved step.', 'The oxalomalate sequence provides a valid proposed route to the C5 hydroxy-keto acid, but selective capture and downstream reduction are not established as an integrated process. Replacing hydroxylamine completion with Fe0/ammonia introduces an additional mechanistic and selectivity uncertainty. Explicitly recognizing these uncertainties is appropriate, but does not establish productive glutamate synthesis.', \"General-base-promoted pyruvate-glyoxylate aldol chemistry is plausible and broadly consistent with the non-iron chemistry discussed by Springsteen et al. However, 'general base' does not identify an operating catalyst, buffer or pH regime. The route also retains the unresolved selective reduction in rxn_007. Its hydroxylamine completion is chemically stronger than the corresponding proposed ammonia implementation.\", 'The proposed sequence is stoichiometrically valid, but combines an underspecified base-promoted entry with two insufficiently validated selective reductions. Separate pH stages are legitimate; the concern is the absence of an operational protocol demonstrating suitable speciation and substrate survival, not the use or ordering of multiple stages.', 'The declared aldehyde, cyanohydrin and aminodinitrile have the correct connectivity to yield glutamate. Treating cyanohydrin amination as Strecker equilibration is more credible than assuming direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group. Patel et al. (2015, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202) provides relevant framework-level support, but productive double-nitrile hydrolysis under the proposed staging is not established here. Amide accumulation and cyclic amidine or lactam products could delay or divert free glutamate formation.', 'Direct Strecker formation of the stated aminodinitrile is chemically plausible and leads to the correct glutamate skeleton. Its principal unresolved target-forming step is the same double-nitrile hydrolysis used in P9. Imine-first and cyanohydrin-first descriptions can represent overlapping equilibria; neither ordering alone establishes additional experimental validation. Acidic hydrolysis is a reasonable proposal, but conditions and free-glutamate yield are required.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glutamate, racemic D/L, represented by the neutral glutamic-acid reference structure\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Construct the C5 skeleton through pyruvate\u2013glyoxylate aldol chemistry or oxaloacetate\u2013glyoxylate addition followed by nonoxidative decarboxylation, then install nitrogen through hydroxylamine condensation and iron-mediated oxime reduction. Ten distinct reaction sequences are listed; they are precursor-entry and catalyst variants, not ten independently demonstrated synthesis families. The feedstock boundary permits supplied pyruvate, glyoxylate, oxaloacetate, isocitrate, hydroxylamine, water, acid equivalents and iron, with an optional inorganic-carbon entry. All sequences belong to one connected bipartite graph. Chemistry reported at 343 K is assigned to surface geothermal water rather than forced into the specified hotter hydrothermal range. Except where explicitly identified as reported, numerical concentrations, pH values and residence times are proposed screening conditions, not published protocols or measured yields. The shared selective alkene-reduction step remains an explicitly speculative implementation; consequently this is a complete candidate network relative to its declared feedstocks, not an experimentally established end-to-end synthesis. Unverified FeS/H2 ammonia amination, poorly constrained double-nitrile hydrolysis and the review's carbon-unbalanced C6 decomposition are omitted. Molecular formulas use neutral reference forms for organic acids; actual aqueous protonation depends on pH. Stoichiometric coefficients are stated in mechanisms without repeating molecule IDs in input or output arrays. No molecular oxygen or enantioselective process is assumed.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"water\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyruvic acid; pyruvate reference form\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxylic acid; glyoxylate reference form\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H4O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxobutanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"oxaloacetic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H4O5/c5-2(4(8)9)1-3(6)7/h1H2,(H,6,7)(H,8,9)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H8O7\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1-hydroxypropane-1,2,3-tricarboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"isocitric acid, stereochemistry unspecified\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H6O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"butanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"succinic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H6O4/c5-3(6)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,5,6)(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6O6\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxy-4-oxopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H6O6/c6-2(4(8)9)1-3(7)5(10)11/h2,6H,1H2,(H,8,9)(H,10,11)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H6O8\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-carboxy-2-hydroxy-4-oxopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"3-oxalomalic acid; oxalomalate reference form\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C6H6O8/c7-2(5(11)12)1(4(9)10)3(8)6(13)14/h1-2,7H,(H,9,10)(H,11,12)(H,13,14)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H4O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"unsaturated oxoglutaric acid, alkene geometry unspecified\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H4O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"2-oxoglutaric acid; alpha-ketoglutarate reference form\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H6O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H7NO5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(hydroxyimino)pentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"2-oxoglutarate oxime, E/Z unspecified\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"elemental iron; Fe(0)\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe+2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(2+)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferrous ion, one iron atom with charge +2\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe/q+2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"H+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"proton; aqueous acid-equivalent bookkeeping species\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/p+1\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glutamic acid; racemic glutamate reference form\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO4/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h3H,1-2,6H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Mineral-promoted inorganic-carbon entry to pyruvate, aggregate branch\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Awaruite, Ni3Fe: candidate native-alloy surface for hydrogen activation and carbon-dioxide reduction\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"373 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Proposed 50 bar total; approximately 35 bar H2 and 15 bar CO2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Awaruite, Ni3Fe\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening window: in-situ pH 7.5-9.0, approximately 10 g/L crushed alloy and 24-72 h residence. These are not attributed as a published protocol. Anoxic rock-water reactor. For downstream surface chemistry, test a co-located shallow hydrothermal outflow with cooling and depressurization within approximately 1 h; measure pyruvate recovery before assigning productive flux.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aggregate target-producing branch, not an elementary or selective reaction: 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> pyruvic acid + 3 H2O. Surface-mediated reduction and C-C assembly are lumped because the supplied evidence does not establish every intermediate. Other carbon products are expected but are outside this target branch.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Preiner et al. (2020), as summarized in the supplied review, supports mineral-mediated CO2/H2 chemistry producing pyruvate. It does not establish the exact proposed alloy loading, gas composition, residence time or recovered pyruvate concentration. Awaruite is chemically justified by hydrogen activation; an unspecified mineral is not substituted for an operating catalyst. This optional entry remains conditional on measured C3 production and survival during discharge. Trace detection alone would not establish supply to the millimolar downstream screening stages, and no unmodeled concentration step or quantitative transport recovery is assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxaloacetate beta-keto-acid decarboxylation to pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Dissolved iron(II): Lewis-acid promoter of beta-keto-acid decarboxylation\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: 5-20 mM oxaloacetate, 1-5 mM Fe2+, pH 5.0-6.0, and 0.25-6 h residence. Use an anoxic, mineral-covered geothermal pool or an equivalent laboratory analogue. Track pyruvate and competing decomposition products; introduce glyoxylate after establishing pyruvate formation when this entry is intended.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Oxaloacetic acid -> pyruvic acid + CO2. Beta-keto-acid decarboxylation produces the pyruvate enol, followed by tautomerization. All coefficients are one.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1) supports iron-promoted oxaloacetate-to-pyruvate chemistry. The reaction is structurally well founded and requires neither an oxidant nor a reductant. The proposed concentration and residence-time window is not a reported yield optimization. This branch competes directly with oxaloacetate capture in rxn_006, so the two entries require different staging rather than assumed simultaneous high selectivity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Isocitrate retro-aldol cleavage supplying glyoxylate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Dissolved iron(II): coordination and Lewis-acid promotion of carbon-carbon cleavage\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: 5-20 mM isocitrate, 1-5 mM Fe2+, pH 5.0-6.0, and 1-24 h residence. Maintain anoxia and mineral or water-column UV shielding. Quantify both glyoxylate and succinate rather than inferring cleavage from disappearance of isocitrate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Isocitric acid -> glyoxylic acid + succinic acid. Retro-aldol C-C cleavage and proton transfer conserve all six carbon atoms; succinate is an explicit coproduct, not a hidden carbon loss.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The supplied account of Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1) identifies iron-promoted isocitrate cleavage as a glyoxylate source. Its exact isolated-reaction kinetics and selectivity are not provided. This is an alternative inlet using a declared C6 organic feedstock, not a claim that isocitrate itself has been synthesized from inorganic carbon here. Glyoxylate capture must compete with its further reaction or degradation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron(II)-promoted pyruvate-glyoxylate aldol addition\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Dissolved iron(II): Lewis-acid coordination of the carbonyl substrates\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"The 343 K temperature follows the supplied report. Proposed screen: 10-25 mM pyruvate, 10-25 mM glyoxylate, 1-5 mM Fe2+, pH 5.0-6.0, and 1-24 h residence. Keep the aqueous phase anoxic and UV-shielded. Test actual upstream effluents alongside authenticated-substrate controls; nominal feed concentrations are not assumed to emerge automatically from rxn_001.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pyruvic acid + glyoxylic acid -> 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid. The pyruvate enol or enolate equivalent attacks the glyoxylate aldehyde. This is aldol addition: no water is released in this node.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1) provides the principal support for this C3+C2 skeleton-building chemistry. Springsteen et al. (2018, DOI: 10.1038/s41467-017-02591-0) supports related glyoxylate aldol networks. Fe2+ is used as a Lewis acid, not treated as an inexhaustible electron source. Product accumulation and carbon recovery must be measured because reversible aldol chemistry and competing reactions can prevent useful downstream flux.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Base-dominated pyruvate-glyoxylate aldol addition\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydroxide supplied by alkaline water-rock buffering: general-base catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide-mediated general-base catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed, unvalidated implementation: pH 9.0-10.0, 10-25 mM of each organic substrate, and 1-7 days residence in an anoxic, covered alkaline pool. No Fe2+ is deliberately added. Iron-bearing upstream effluents require a separately tested precipitation/settling stage before this base-dominated comparison; organic losses during that handling cannot be assumed negligible. Downstream acidic iron chemistry requires a separately measured pH transition.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pyruvic acid + glyoxylic acid -> 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid. General-base-assisted enolization and aldol addition give the same connectivity as rxn_004, but through a distinct catalyst regime.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Springsteen et al. (2018, DOI: 10.1038/s41467-017-02591-0) supports non-iron glyoxylate aldol chemistry at the reaction-family level. The supplied material does not establish this exact hydroxide-only implementation; its rate and selectivity are therefore speculative. It is included because alkaline waters provide a chemically meaningful alternative to soluble-iron catalysis, not as an independently demonstrated glutamate synthesis family. Pyruvate enolization, competing condensations and substrate survival over the longer residence time are explicit bottlenecks.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxaloacetate-glyoxylate aldol capture to oxalomalate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"296-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: freshly supplied 5-10 mM oxaloacetate, 10-30 mM glyoxylate, pH 7.0-8.5, and 0.25-3 h residence. No added metal catalyst. Use cool, anoxic, UV-shielded water to limit premature oxaloacetate loss. Measure oxalomalate and pyruvate in parallel.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Oxaloacetic acid + glyoxylic acid -> 3-oxalomalic acid. Aldol attack from the activated oxaloacetate methylene builds the C6 beta-hydroxy, beta-keto tricarboxylic-acid skeleton. All coefficients are one.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Springsteen et al. (2018, DOI: 10.1038/s41467-017-02591-0) provides relevant support for the oxalomalate branch of glyoxylate reaction networks. The exact proposed screening window is not a supplied experimental protocol. Capture must outrun rxn_002 and other oxaloacetate losses; this competition cannot be resolved merely by drawing both reactions. No peroxide-dependent oxidative cycle is imported into this anoxic branch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Nonoxidative oxalomalate decarboxylation to the C5 hydroxy-keto acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"296-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"The supplied review reports approximately 296 K and 3 h. Proposed replication window: pH 6.5-8.0 and 1-6 h, with no oxidant. After confirming C5 product formation, warm the same catchment water to the 343 K geothermal stage and measure recovery during the temperature and pH transition.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"3-Oxalomalic acid -> 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid + CO2. Loss of the beta-keto carboxyl group followed by proton transfer is nonoxidative decarboxylation. All coefficients are one.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Springsteen et al. (2018, DOI: 10.1038/s41467-017-02591-0) supports this entry to the C5 intermediate according to the supplied review. Calling this particular balanced transformation oxidative would incorrectly imply a missing electron acceptor. Its integration with the warmer downstream iron chemistry has not been demonstrated by the supplied evidence, so intermediate recovery rather than assumed quantitative transfer is required.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydroxy-keto-acid dehydration to the conjugated unsaturated C5 intermediate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Dissolved iron(II): Lewis-acid promoter of dehydration\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: 1-10 mM hydroxy-keto acid, 1-5 mM Fe2+, pH 5.0-6.0, and 0.5-12 h residence. Maintain anoxia and UV shielding. Follow hydroxy-keto acid, unsaturated product and decomposition products over time instead of assuming complete dehydration in bulk water.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"4-Hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid -> 4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid + H2O. Beta-elimination creates the conjugated alkene. Alkene geometry is not assigned without analytical evidence.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1) supports the reported iron-promoted reaction family. The numerical pH, loading and residence-time ranges here are proposed tests. Rehydration and decomposition compete in water; the reported network does not justify assuming quantitative accumulation of an isolated unsaturated intermediate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Explicit sacrificial-iron reduction of the C5 alkene, conditional implementation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Elemental iron, explicitly represented as mol_015: stoichiometric electron donor, not an inexhaustible catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed selectivity screen: 1-5 mM unsaturated substrate, 2-5 molar equivalents of Fe0, pH 5.0-6.0 maintained by an explicit acid reservoir, and 0.25-6 h residence. Compare freshly exposed and passivated native-iron surfaces; report reactive area and iron dissolution. Remove solids after the selected residence time. Quantify keto-group reduction, decarboxylation and corrosion-derived H2 as competing processes.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced proposed net reaction: 4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid + Fe0 + 2 H+ -> 2-oxoglutaric acid + Fe2+. Two-electron, two-proton reduction saturates the conjugated C=C bond while the desired branch retains the keto group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE IMPLEMENTATION. Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1) supports formation of 2-oxoglutarate within iron-promoted chemistry, but the supplied material does not establish selective reduction of this isolated alkene with the specified Fe0 treatment. Explicit iron consumption repairs the missing electron budget without pretending to establish kinetics. This is the decisive shared bottleneck for all ten sequences: useful target production requires demonstrating keto-group retention and measurable 2-oxoglutarate recovery in the actual upstream mixture. Lee et al. (2014, DOI: 10.1016/j.gca.2014.02.043) additionally motivates controlling thermal exposure and decomposition. No successful yield is asserted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydroxylamine condensation to the 2-oxoglutarate oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed staged screen: 1-10 mM 2-oxoglutarate, 1.1-2 equivalents of hydroxylamine, pH 4.5-6.0, and 0.5-4 h before adding fresh Fe0 for rxn_011. No added catalyst or oxygen is required. Measure oxime formation and residual hydroxylamine; do not presume isolation or quantitative conversion.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"2-Oxoglutaric acid + NH2OH -> 2-(hydroxyimino)pentanedioic acid + H2O. Carbonyl addition gives a carbinolamine that dehydrates to an oxime. This is oxime formation, not transamination.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1) supports hydroxylamine-assisted conversion of keto acids to amino acids. Resolving that overall transformation into an oxime node is a mechanistic interpretation, not a claim that the supplied review reports an independently optimized isolated-oxime experiment. Hydroxylamine availability remains an unresolved feedstock constraint. No oxygen-dependent ammonia oxidation is introduced to supply it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Four-electron oxime reduction to racemic glutamate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Elemental iron, explicitly represented as mol_015: sacrificial reductant for oxime reduction\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: retain the oxime-containing solution from rxn_010, add 4-10 molar equivalents of fresh Fe0 relative to measured oxime, maintain pH 4.5-6.0, and sample over 1-24 h. The balanced minimum is two Fe atoms per oxime; excess accommodates competing corrosion and nitrogen-donor reduction. After reaction, remove iron solids and cool promptly. Quantify free glutamate, residual oxime, nitrogen-containing side products and total carbon recovery with authenticated analytical standards.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced net reaction: 2-(hydroxyimino)pentanedioic acid + 2 Fe0 + 4 H+ -> glutamic acid + 2 Fe2+ + H2O. Four electrons reduce the C=N functionality and cleave the N-O bond. Achiral conditions provide no basis for selecting the L enantiomer.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1) provides the strongest supplied support for the combined hydroxylamine/iron amino-acid-forming stage. The explicit four-electron balance avoids mislabeling iron as solely catalytic. The proposed staged dosing, particle reactivity and residence time are not supplied primary protocols, and free-glutamate yield must be measured rather than inferred from oxime disappearance. Competing iron passivation, hydroxylamine reduction and organic decomposition remain relevant. End-to-end validation requires quantified glutamate from each full sequence, not confidence extrapolated from the number of pathway variants.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied pyruvate and glyoxylate through iron-promoted aldol chemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"The shortest principal candidate from declared C3 and C2 organic inlets. It uses the best-supported carbon-assembly family and hydroxylamine completion, but remains conditional on selective rxn_009 reduction and measured full-sequence recovery.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Inorganic-carbon-derived pyruvate with iron-promoted aldol capture\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal CO2/H2 chemistry supplies the C3 partner; glyoxylate is a declared external inlet. Cooling, discharge, pyruvate recovery and compatible concentrations must be demonstrated before this branch can be considered productive.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxaloacetate decarboxylation followed by iron-promoted aldol capture\",\n      \"description\": \"Oxaloacetate first supplies pyruvate; subsequently added glyoxylate supplies the C2 partner. Staging distinguishes this route from direct oxaloacetate capture in P9.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Isocitrate-derived glyoxylate with iron-promoted aldol capture\",\n      \"description\": \"Isocitrate cleavage supplies glyoxylate and explicitly releases succinate. Pyruvate is the external C3 inlet. This avoids assuming that glyoxylate must originate from a particular photochemical framework.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied pyruvate and glyoxylate through base-dominated aldol chemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"An alkaline catalyst-regime alternative to P1. It shares the downstream chemistry but additionally depends on validating hydroxide-promoted aldol kinetics and recovery during the subsequent pH transition.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Inorganic-carbon-derived pyruvate through base-dominated aldol chemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"Combines the optional CO2/H2 C3 entry with an alkaline aldol stage and externally supplied glyoxylate. It inherits both inorganic-carbon delivery uncertainties and the unvalidated base-dominated implementation; it is not an independently demonstrated synthesis family.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxaloacetate-derived pyruvate through base-dominated aldol chemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"Oxaloacetate decarboxylation precedes alkaline pyruvate-glyoxylate capture. Ferrous-ion depletion and organic retention during the change of catalyst regime require direct measurement rather than assumed compatibility.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Isocitrate-derived glyoxylate through base-dominated aldol chemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"Isocitrate supplies glyoxylate, which reacts with externally supplied pyruvate in the alkaline variant. Removal of iron from the cleavage effluent may remove organics, so this route has an additional experimentally unresolved handling requirement.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct oxaloacetate-glyoxylate capture through oxalomalate\",\n      \"description\": \"A distinct skeleton-building sequence captures glyoxylate before oxaloacetate decarboxylation. The resulting C6 intermediate loses CO2 nonoxidatively to reach the common C5 hydroxy-keto acid. Productive capture must compete with the entry used in P3.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Isocitrate-derived glyoxylate entering the oxalomalate route\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyoxylate released from isocitrate is captured by externally supplied oxaloacetate, then decarboxylated to the common C5 intermediate. The cleavage effluent must be cooled and its effects on oxaloacetate lifetime tested before the capture stage.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_014\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_015\",\n    \"mol_016\",\n    \"mol_017\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glutamate, racemic D/L, represented by the neutral glutamic-acid reference structure", "overall_strategy": "Construct the C5 skeleton through pyruvate\u2013glyoxylate aldol chemistry or oxaloacetate\u2013glyoxylate addition followed by nonoxidative decarboxylation, then install nitrogen through hydroxylamine condensation and iron-mediated oxime reduction. Ten distinct reaction sequences are listed; they are precursor-entry and catalyst variants, not ten independently demonstrated synthesis families. The feedstock boundary permits supplied pyruvate, glyoxylate, oxaloacetate, isocitrate, hydroxylamine, water, acid equivalents and iron, with an optional inorganic-carbon entry. All sequences belong to one connected bipartite graph. Chemistry reported at 343 K is assigned to surface geothermal water rather than forced into the specified hotter hydrothermal range. Except where explicitly identified as reported, numerical concentrations, pH values and residence times are proposed screening conditions, not published protocols or measured yields. The shared selective alkene-reduction step remains an explicitly speculative implementation; consequently this is a complete candidate network relative to its declared feedstocks, not an experimentally established end-to-end synthesis. Unverified FeS/H2 ammonia amination, poorly constrained double-nitrile hydrolysis and the review's carbon-unbalanced C6 decomposition are omitted. Molecular formulas use neutral reference forms for organic acids; actual aqueous protonation depends on pH. Stoichiometric coefficients are stated in mechanisms without repeating molecule IDs in input or output arrays. No molecular oxygen or enantioselective process is assumed.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "water", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvic acid; pyruvate reference form", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C2H2O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxoacetic acid", "common_name": "glyoxylic acid; glyoxylate reference form", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C4H4O5", "iupac_name": "2-oxobutanedioic acid", "common_name": "oxaloacetic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H4O5/c5-2(4(8)9)1-3(6)7/h1H2,(H,6,7)(H,8,9)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C6H8O7", "iupac_name": "1-hydroxypropane-1,2,3-tricarboxylic acid", "common_name": "isocitric acid, stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C4H6O4", "iupac_name": "butanedioic acid", "common_name": "succinic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H6O4/c5-3(6)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,5,6)(H,7,8)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C5H6O6", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxy-4-oxopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H6O6/c6-2(4(8)9)1-3(7)5(10)11/h2,6H,1H2,(H,8,9)(H,10,11)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C6H6O8", "iupac_name": "3-carboxy-2-hydroxy-4-oxopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "3-oxalomalic acid; oxalomalate reference form", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C6H6O8/c7-2(5(11)12)1(4(9)10)3(8)6(13)14/h1-2,7H,(H,9,10)(H,11,12)(H,13,14)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C5H4O5", "iupac_name": "4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid", "common_name": "unsaturated oxoglutaric acid, alkene geometry unspecified", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H4O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C5H6O5", "iupac_name": "2-oxopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "2-oxoglutaric acid; alpha-ketoglutarate reference form", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H6O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "H3NO", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": "hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C5H7NO5", "iupac_name": "2-(hydroxyimino)pentanedioic acid", "common_name": "2-oxoglutarate oxime, E/Z unspecified", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "iron", "common_name": "elemental iron; Fe(0)", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "Fe+2", "iupac_name": "iron(2+)", "common_name": "ferrous ion, one iron atom with charge +2", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "hydron", "common_name": "proton; aqueous acid-equivalent bookkeeping species", "inchi": "InChI=1S/p+1", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C5H9NO4", "iupac_name": "2-aminopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "glutamic acid; racemic glutamate reference form", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO4/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h3H,1-2,6H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Mineral-promoted inorganic-carbon entry to pyruvate, aggregate branch", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_004", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Awaruite, Ni3Fe: candidate native-alloy surface for hydrogen activation and carbon-dioxide reduction"], "conditions": {"temperature": "373 K", "pressure": "Proposed 50 bar total; approximately 35 bar H2 and 15 bar CO2", "catalyst": "Awaruite, Ni3Fe", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening window: in-situ pH 7.5-9.0, approximately 10 g/L crushed alloy and 24-72 h residence. These are not attributed as a published protocol. Anoxic rock-water reactor. For downstream surface chemistry, test a co-located shallow hydrothermal outflow with cooling and depressurization within approximately 1 h; measure pyruvate recovery before assigning productive flux."}, "mechanism": "Aggregate target-producing branch, not an elementary or selective reaction: 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> pyruvic acid + 3 H2O. Surface-mediated reduction and C-C assembly are lumped because the supplied evidence does not establish every intermediate. Other carbon products are expected but are outside this target branch.", "reasoning": "Preiner et al. (2020), as summarized in the supplied review, supports mineral-mediated CO2/H2 chemistry producing pyruvate. It does not establish the exact proposed alloy loading, gas composition, residence time or recovered pyruvate concentration. Awaruite is chemically justified by hydrogen activation; an unspecified mineral is not substituted for an operating catalyst. This optional entry remains conditional on measured C3 production and survival during discharge. Trace detection alone would not establish supply to the millimolar downstream screening stages, and no unmodeled concentration step or quantitative transport recovery is assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Oxaloacetate beta-keto-acid decarboxylation to pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_004", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved iron(II): Lewis-acid promoter of beta-keto-acid decarboxylation"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Aqueous Fe2+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: 5-20 mM oxaloacetate, 1-5 mM Fe2+, pH 5.0-6.0, and 0.25-6 h residence. Use an anoxic, mineral-covered geothermal pool or an equivalent laboratory analogue. Track pyruvate and competing decomposition products; introduce glyoxylate after establishing pyruvate formation when this entry is intended."}, "mechanism": "Oxaloacetic acid -> pyruvic acid + CO2. Beta-keto-acid decarboxylation produces the pyruvate enol, followed by tautomerization. All coefficients are one.", "reasoning": "Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1) supports iron-promoted oxaloacetate-to-pyruvate chemistry. The reaction is structurally well founded and requires neither an oxidant nor a reductant. The proposed concentration and residence-time window is not a reported yield optimization. This branch competes directly with oxaloacetate capture in rxn_006, so the two entries require different staging rather than assumed simultaneous high selectivity."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Isocitrate retro-aldol cleavage supplying glyoxylate", "inputs": ["mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved iron(II): coordination and Lewis-acid promotion of carbon-carbon cleavage"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Aqueous Fe2+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: 5-20 mM isocitrate, 1-5 mM Fe2+, pH 5.0-6.0, and 1-24 h residence. Maintain anoxia and mineral or water-column UV shielding. Quantify both glyoxylate and succinate rather than inferring cleavage from disappearance of isocitrate."}, "mechanism": "Isocitric acid -> glyoxylic acid + succinic acid. Retro-aldol C-C cleavage and proton transfer conserve all six carbon atoms; succinate is an explicit coproduct, not a hidden carbon loss.", "reasoning": "The supplied account of Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1) identifies iron-promoted isocitrate cleavage as a glyoxylate source. Its exact isolated-reaction kinetics and selectivity are not provided. This is an alternative inlet using a declared C6 organic feedstock, not a claim that isocitrate itself has been synthesized from inorganic carbon here. Glyoxylate capture must compete with its further reaction or degradation."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Iron(II)-promoted pyruvate-glyoxylate aldol addition", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved iron(II): Lewis-acid coordination of the carbonyl substrates"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Aqueous Fe2+", "aqueous": true, "other": "The 343 K temperature follows the supplied report. Proposed screen: 10-25 mM pyruvate, 10-25 mM glyoxylate, 1-5 mM Fe2+, pH 5.0-6.0, and 1-24 h residence. Keep the aqueous phase anoxic and UV-shielded. Test actual upstream effluents alongside authenticated-substrate controls; nominal feed concentrations are not assumed to emerge automatically from rxn_001."}, "mechanism": "Pyruvic acid + glyoxylic acid -> 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid. The pyruvate enol or enolate equivalent attacks the glyoxylate aldehyde. This is aldol addition: no water is released in this node.", "reasoning": "Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1) provides the principal support for this C3+C2 skeleton-building chemistry. Springsteen et al. (2018, DOI: 10.1038/s41467-017-02591-0) supports related glyoxylate aldol networks. Fe2+ is used as a Lewis acid, not treated as an inexhaustible electron source. Product accumulation and carbon recovery must be measured because reversible aldol chemistry and competing reactions can prevent useful downstream flux."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Base-dominated pyruvate-glyoxylate aldol addition", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide supplied by alkaline water-rock buffering: general-base catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-343 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydroxide-mediated general-base catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed, unvalidated implementation: pH 9.0-10.0, 10-25 mM of each organic substrate, and 1-7 days residence in an anoxic, covered alkaline pool. No Fe2+ is deliberately added. Iron-bearing upstream effluents require a separately tested precipitation/settling stage before this base-dominated comparison; organic losses during that handling cannot be assumed negligible. Downstream acidic iron chemistry requires a separately measured pH transition."}, "mechanism": "Pyruvic acid + glyoxylic acid -> 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid. General-base-assisted enolization and aldol addition give the same connectivity as rxn_004, but through a distinct catalyst regime.", "reasoning": "Springsteen et al. (2018, DOI: 10.1038/s41467-017-02591-0) supports non-iron glyoxylate aldol chemistry at the reaction-family level. The supplied material does not establish this exact hydroxide-only implementation; its rate and selectivity are therefore speculative. It is included because alkaline waters provide a chemically meaningful alternative to soluble-iron catalysis, not as an independently demonstrated glutamate synthesis family. Pyruvate enolization, competing condensations and substrate survival over the longer residence time are explicit bottlenecks."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Oxaloacetate-glyoxylate aldol capture to oxalomalate", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "296-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: freshly supplied 5-10 mM oxaloacetate, 10-30 mM glyoxylate, pH 7.0-8.5, and 0.25-3 h residence. No added metal catalyst. Use cool, anoxic, UV-shielded water to limit premature oxaloacetate loss. Measure oxalomalate and pyruvate in parallel."}, "mechanism": "Oxaloacetic acid + glyoxylic acid -> 3-oxalomalic acid. Aldol attack from the activated oxaloacetate methylene builds the C6 beta-hydroxy, beta-keto tricarboxylic-acid skeleton. All coefficients are one.", "reasoning": "Springsteen et al. (2018, DOI: 10.1038/s41467-017-02591-0) provides relevant support for the oxalomalate branch of glyoxylate reaction networks. The exact proposed screening window is not a supplied experimental protocol. Capture must outrun rxn_002 and other oxaloacetate losses; this competition cannot be resolved merely by drawing both reactions. No peroxide-dependent oxidative cycle is imported into this anoxic branch."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Nonoxidative oxalomalate decarboxylation to the C5 hydroxy-keto acid", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "296-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "The supplied review reports approximately 296 K and 3 h. Proposed replication window: pH 6.5-8.0 and 1-6 h, with no oxidant. After confirming C5 product formation, warm the same catchment water to the 343 K geothermal stage and measure recovery during the temperature and pH transition."}, "mechanism": "3-Oxalomalic acid -> 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid + CO2. Loss of the beta-keto carboxyl group followed by proton transfer is nonoxidative decarboxylation. All coefficients are one.", "reasoning": "Springsteen et al. (2018, DOI: 10.1038/s41467-017-02591-0) supports this entry to the C5 intermediate according to the supplied review. Calling this particular balanced transformation oxidative would incorrectly imply a missing electron acceptor. Its integration with the warmer downstream iron chemistry has not been demonstrated by the supplied evidence, so intermediate recovery rather than assumed quantitative transfer is required."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydroxy-keto-acid dehydration to the conjugated unsaturated C5 intermediate", "inputs": ["mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved iron(II): Lewis-acid promote