proton reduction saturates the conjugated C=C bond while the desired branch retains the keto group.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE IMPLEMENTATION. Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1) supports formation of 2-oxoglutarate within iron-promoted chemistry, but the supplied material does not establish selective reduction of this isolated alkene with the specified Fe0 treatment. Explicit iron consumption repairs the missing electron budget without pretending to establish kinetics. This is the decisive shared bottleneck for all ten sequences: useful target production requires demonstrating keto-group retention and measurable 2-oxoglutarate recovery in the actual upstream mixture. Lee et al. (2014, DOI: 10.1016/j.gca.2014.02.043) additionally motivates controlling thermal exposure and decomposition. No successful yield is asserted."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hydroxylamine condensation to the 2-oxoglutarate oxime", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-343 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed staged screen: 1-10 mM 2-oxoglutarate, 1.1-2 equivalents of hydroxylamine, pH 4.5-6.0, and 0.5-4 h before adding fresh Fe0 for rxn_011. No added catalyst or oxygen is required. Measure oxime formation and residual hydroxylamine; do not presume isolation or quantitative conversion."}, "mechanism": "2-Oxoglutaric acid + NH2OH -> 2-(hydroxyimino)pentanedioic acid + H2O. Carbonyl addition gives a carbinolamine that dehydrates to an oxime. This is oxime formation, not transamination.", "reasoning": "Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019, DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1151-1) supports hydroxylamine-assisted conversion of keto acids to amino acids. Resolving that overall transformation into an oxime node is a mechanistic interpretation, not a claim that the supplied review reports an independently optimized isolate